<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<RootNamespace>KatLang</RootNamespace>

		<PackageId>KatLang</PackageId>
		<Version>0.8.172</Version>
		<Authors>katlangorg</Authors>
		<Company>Logics Research Centre SIA</Company>
		<Copyright>Copyright © Logics Research Centre SIA and contributors</Copyright>

		<Description>KatLang is a domain-specific programming language for calculations. This package provides the core parser, evaluator, and semantic model.</Description>
		<PackageTitle>KatLang</PackageTitle>
		<PackageTags>katlang;dsl;calculations;mathematics;physics</PackageTags>

		<PackageProjectUrl>https://katlang.org</PackageProjectUrl>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/katlangorg/katlangnet</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>

		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE</PackageLicenseFile>

		<IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
		<SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
		<PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>

		<PackageReleaseNotes>Adds the omitted-dot spelling of the graced dot edge: in the eligible parser context — a bare-name postfix Grace run followed by a same-line member identifier — `a~t` builds the same ordinary graced DotCall as `a~.t` (identical semantic raw shape; source spans differ) and elaborates to the same edge as `a.~t`. The edge keeps ordinary STRUCTURAL-FIRST DotCall semantics: `K = a~t` infers `(t, a)`, and the direct call `t(a)` describes only its lexical-fallback behavior — a receiver that owns the member structurally resolves to that member, so `K = a~t` is not universally equivalent to `K(t, a) = t(a)`. The shorthand is grammar-local, not a blanket textual rewrite of `~` to `~.` (for example, an indexing selector consumes one primary, so `x:a~t` selects with the whole edge while `x:a~.t` dot-chains the index result). The member identifier must follow the postfix Grace run on the same physical line; an identifier that begins a declaration keeps its declaration meaning (`a~ K = 5` stays a graced row plus a definition), a newline still separates rows, and compound receivers keep their existing adjacency parse (`(x + y)~t` remains two slots). COMPATIBILITY: `A~B` (a postfix-graced name followed by a same-line identifier) previously parsed as two adjacent expression slots and now denotes the compact graced dot edge — write `A~, B` when the graced value and the following value are intended as separate slots, mirroring the spread marker's `A*, B` rule. No new AST shape, operator, evaluation rule, or argument ordering is introduced; parameter inference, elaboration, and runtime behavior are exactly those of the dotted spellings.</PackageReleaseNotes>

		<Title>KatLang</Title>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="KatLang.Tests" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="KatLang.Benchmarks" />
		<!-- Grants the standalone parser fuzzing harness (fuzz/KatLang.ParserFuzz)
		     access to the internal Parser.ParseSyntax raw-syntax entry point.
		     Compile-time friend access only; does not change any public API. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="KatLang.ParserFuzz" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
	  <None Include="..\..\LICENSE">
	    <Pack>True</Pack>
	    <PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
	  </None>
	  <None Include="..\..\README.md">
	    <Pack>True</Pack>
	    <PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
	  </None>
	  <None Include="..\..\NOTICE">
		<Pack>True</Pack>
		<PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
	  </None>
	  <None Include="..\..\PATENTS">
		<Pack>True</Pack>
		<PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
	  </None>
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
